ely (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$"] [17592186134139 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=2xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186134140 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186134368 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=2x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186134369 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}\\pi z+2xz)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nEvaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,0).$"] [17592186134370 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$, what is $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$?"] [17592186134371 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\operatorname{cos}yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\operatorname{ln}xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1, \\frac{π}{2}, 1)$."] [17592186134376 "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [17592186134377 "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation."] [17592186134378 "![](images/6f/6f51a25843259881994e3dca0781b780878bec349930b76fbf449dc824e69ec2.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186134386 "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)=2\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region."] [17592186167050 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186167090 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2ay\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186172280 "Determine the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186172281 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186172301 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186172302 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186172303 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186172304 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\hspace{0.222em}3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186172305 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$"] [17592186172306 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186172307 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186172308 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+1,\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186172309 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186172675 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xyz+{y}^{2}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,-1)$ to $(2,1,1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$"] [17592186176231 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\