r field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through the curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186180039 "![](images/27/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186180040 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186180041 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$"] [17592186198593 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨z-y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186198594 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$"] [17592186198602 